---
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro'
import TextPage from '../layouts/TextPage.astro'

/**
 * Short on purpose. With no account, no analytics and no database, a long
 * policy is padding around four true sentences. What survives is only what a
 * reader could be surprised by: the update check, the two third parties this
 * page touches, and email.
 */
const updatedLabel = '15 September 2026'
---

<TextPage
  title="Privacy policy | SpreadPaper"
  description="No accounts, no analytics, no cookies, no database. The whole privacy policy fits on one screen."
  socialDescription="No accounts, no analytics, no cookies, no database. The shortest privacy policy you will read today."
  eyebrow="Privacy"
  heading="There is nothing to see here"
  lead="No accounts, no analytics, no cookies, no database. Nothing to opt out of, because there was never anything to opt into."
  updated={updatedLabel}
>
  <Prose>
    <h2>Your photos stay put</h2>
    <p>
      The app is sandboxed, so it sees the files you hand it and nothing else. Your wallpapers are
      rendered on your Mac, to your own disk.
    </p>

    <h2>The app phones home once</h2>
    <p>
      It asks GitHub whether a newer version exists. GitHub sees your IP address and the app's
      version, the same as every web server you have ever met. Block <code class="cd-code"
        >api.github.com</code
      > and even that stops. It lives in <a
        href="https://github.com/spreadpaper/SpreadPaper/blob/main/SpreadPaper/Services/UpdateChecker.swift"
        >UpdateChecker.swift</a
      >, if you would rather check than trust.
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      Static, served by Cloudflare, typeface from Google Fonts. Both of them see your IP address.
      That is two third parties, which is two more than I wanted and rather fewer than the last page
      you had open.
    </p>

    <h2>If you email me</h2>
    <p>
      Write to <a href="mailto:hello@spreadpaper.app">hello@spreadpaper.app</a> and I will know your
      email address. That is how email works. I keep it while the conversation is useful. If you are
      in the EU, the GDPR lets you ask what personal data is held about you, and the answer is: that
      email.
    </p>

    <h2>The small print</h2>
    <p>
      Run from Curaçao, Dutch Caribbean, by <a href="https://robinvanbaalen.nl">Robin van Baalen</a>. If any of this changes, so does the
      date at the top. See also the <a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a>.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</TextPage>
